      "text": "1. To determine the number of bounces it takes for the ball to rise less than 30 cm, we start by analyzing the height after each bounce.\n2. The initial height from which the ball is dropped is 243 cm.\n3. The ball bounces back up to $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the height from which it falls. Therefore, after each bounce, the ball rises to $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the previous height.\n\nLet's denote the height of the ball after the $n$-th bounce as $h_n$.\n\nStarting with $h_0 = 243$ cm:\n\\[ h_1 = \\frac{2}{3} \\times 243 = 162 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\\[ h_2 = \\frac{2}{3} \\times 162 = 108 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\\[ h_3 = \\frac{2}{3} \\times 108 = 72 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\\[ h_4 = \\frac{2}{3} \\times 72 = 48 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\\[ h_5 = \\frac{2}{3} \\times 48 = 32 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\\[ h_6 = \\frac{2}{3} \\times 32 = \\frac{64}{3} \\approx 21.33 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest $n$ such that $h_n < 30$ cm.\n- After the 5th bounce, the height $h_5 = 32$ cm, which is greater than 30 cm.\n- After the 6th bounce, the height $h_6 \\approx 21.33$ cm, which is less than 30 cm.\n\nTherefore, the ball first rises less than 30 cm after 6 bounces.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{6}$.",
